exports[`screen: 'help partner servers create' > en 80 plain 1`] = `
"  bladecp partner servers create
 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Creates a server for one of your customers, as your billing system does when
  a service is ordered: a plan and a location of your catalog, the main domain
  and the customer who owns it. On a terminal it asks first; --yes skips the
  question.

  Usage ──────────────────────────────────────
  bladecp partner servers create --plan <id> --location <id> --domain <host>
      --email <email> --name <name> [--external-id <id>]
      [--option <id>=<value>] ... [--component <slug>] ...
      [--contract <months>] [--yes]

  Options ────────────────────────────────────
  --plan <id>                      Plan ID (see "bladecp partner plans")
  --location <id>                  Location ID of the plan (see "bladecp
                                   partner locations")
  --domain <host>                  Main domain of the new server
  --email <email>                  Email of the customer who owns the server
  --name <name>                    Name of the customer who owns the server
  --external-id <id>               The service ID in your billing system, to
                                   find the server later
  --option <id>=<value>            Configurable option and value, by name or ID
                                   (see "bladecp partner options"); for a
                                   quantity, the number. Repeat for more than
                                   one
  --component <slug>[@<version>]   Component to install, with an optional
                                   version (repeat for more than one)
  --contract <months>              Contract term in months (12, 24, 36 or 48)
                                   for plans sold with a contract; records that
                                   the customer accepted the terms
  -y, --yes                        Do not ask for confirmation on a terminal

  With --external-id the server keeps the service ID of your billing system, so
  "bladecp partner servers list --external-id" finds it later.
  Stack Container plans need the components to install (for example --component
  php --component mysql, or a version from "bladecp components list" after @).
  A web server always comes along: NGINX by default, or another one, such as
  --component apache. Other products take none.

  Examples ───────────────────────────────────
  › bladecp partner servers create --plan 12 --location 3 --domain example.com
    --email owner@example.com --name "Example Shop" --external-id 1042
    A server on plan 12, location 3, for a customer
  › bladecp partner servers create --plan 12 --location 3 --domain example.com
    --email owner@example.com --name "Example Shop" --option memory=4
    --component php --component mysql
    With more memory and the PHP component

  Global options ─────────────────────────────
  --json                 Print machine-readable JSON (no colors, no prompts)
  --api-url <url>        API address (default https://api.bladecp.com)
  --lang <en|pt-BR|es>   Language for messages
  --debug                Describe each request on stderr (the key is never
                         shown)
  --verbose              Same as --debug
  -h, --help             Show help
  -v, --version          Show the version

  See also  bladecp partner plans · bladecp partner locations · bladecp partner
  options"
`;

